<?nvs
// ADR 0023's one graph copy, reached through its externalizing carrier —
// `Core\Serialize::encode`/`decode`, § 2's second row.
//
// The four lines this prints are frozen by an exact check in a goal record
// under `data/goals/`; the source is not. Nothing below declares a `__serialize`, a
// `__sleep` or a `__wakeup`, and not because this fixture omits them: § 2
// says no hook fires, so the shape that crosses is exactly the class's own
// declared properties, every time.
class Slot
{
    public int $n = 0;

    public function constructor(int $n)
    {
        $this->n = $n;
    }
}

// § 2: a *graph*, not a tree — a cycle terminates instead of recursing
// forever, which is the case a naive walk hangs on and the reason this class
// is here at all.
class Ring
{
    public int $id = 0;
    public ?Ring $self;

    public function constructor(int $id)
    {
        $this->id = $id;
        $this->self = null;
    }
}

// § 2: the copy is built by direct assignment into the new instance's
// storage and never runs a constructor, so a round trip is an identity on
// the declared properties and on nothing else.
var $blob = Core\Serialize::encode(new Slot(7));
Slot $back = Core\Serialize::decode($blob) as Slot;
if ($back->n == 7) {
    echo 'round-trip ok', "\n";
} else {
    echo 'round-trip lost the value', "\n";
}

// The cycle. `$ring->self` points at `$ring`, so a walk that does not
// remember what it has already seen never returns; what § 2 guarantees is
// that the copy is cyclic in the same place rather than unrolled or cut.
var $ring = new Ring(1);
$ring->self = $ring;
Ring $copy = Core\Serialize::decode(Core\Serialize::encode($ring)) as Ring;
if (($copy->self?->id ?? 0) == 1) {
    echo 'cycle ok', "\n";
} else {
    echo 'cycle was not preserved', "\n";
}

// § 3 and goal item 19: the format is Novis's own, closed and versioned, and
// bytes that are not it are refused *whole*. A partial accept is the type
// confusion `decode` is a `tainted` sink for (01-core-library.md § 13), so
// the refusal is the assertion — not what any field of the result holds.
try {
    Core\Serialize::decode('not novis bytes' as bytes);
    echo 'garbage was accepted', "\n";
} catch (ParseError $e) {
    echo 'refused: not our format', "\n";
}

// Item 19's other half: bytes naming a class whose declared properties no
// longer match are refused whole as well, and this is the case a
// version-tolerant decoder gets wrong by filling the gap with a default.
//
// One program cannot produce these bytes — a file declares `Slot` once — so
// the golden below is an encoding of a `Slot` made under a *different*
// declaration of it: the same class name, with a second `int $extra` beside
// `$n`. It is the format `crates/nvs-runtime/src/graph.rs` writes, byte for
// byte, which is why it is written directly in the code here rather than something this program
// could produce. A version bump there rewrites this line.
var $stale = "NVS\x1b\x01\x09\x04Slot\x02\x01n\x05extra\x02\x07\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x02\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00" as bytes;
try {
    Core\Serialize::decode($stale);
    echo 'a changed shape was accepted', "\n";
} catch (ParseError $e) {
    echo 'refused: shape changed', "\n";
}

// The fixture's own cleanup, and not a property of `Core\Serialize`: Novis
// refcounts and has no cycle collector (`crates/nvs-runtime/src/object.rs`
// § *Decision: no cycle collector*; ADR 0116's *Consequences* says the same for
// an isolate's teardown drain). Both rings above hold their own last reference
// through `$self`, so dropping the locals frees nothing and the process exits
// holding 48 bytes each — which `tools/leak-check.sh` reports as two definite
// losses, correctly. Breaking the cycle by hand is what a program with a
// genuinely cyclic structure has to do until the collector exists.
$ring->self = null;
$copy->self = null;
